    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d59">

                <pb facs="0071" n="59" sameAs="frus:frus1942v04/pg59"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.248/601: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Officer in Chargé at New Delhi</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Merrell</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">New Delhi</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-06-25T09:00:00+05:30">June 25, 1942—9
                            a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received June 25—4:28 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>420. A plane belonging to the Tenth Air Force United States of America in a
                    violent dust storm unwittingly flew over Afghan territory near Jalalabad
                    yesterday between 9:00 and 11:00 a.m. The plane returned to Indian territory as
                    soon as the mistake was discovered. I reported the incident to the Legation at
                    Kabul last night by telegraph and with the acquiescence of the American military
                    authorities here suggested that Thayer<anchor n="22" xml:id="d59-f1"/> in the
                    name of the Government convey at once an appropriate expression of regret for
                    the unavoidable occurrence to the Afghan Government.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Merrell</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d59-f1" n="22" xml:id="d59-f1-content">
                <p>Charles W. Thayer, Chargé in Afghanistan.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
